US-017 is complete. The export endpoint (`GET /api/audit-logs/export?format=xlsx`) already existed from US-008, but its Excel output didn't meet the acceptance criteria — it had only two generic sheets with raw user IDs and locale-dependent date strings. I reworked the backend:

- **`backend/src/api/audit/services/audit.js`** — `exportAuditLogs` now resolves the logs' user IDs to real usernames/emails with a single `$in` query, and precomputes `summary` (total changes, active users, changes by action and by entity type) and `userActivity` (per-user totals, action breakdown, first/last activity). All of it feeds the canonical JSON used for the SHA-256 integrity hash.
- **`backend/src/utils/exportReports.js`** — `generateXLSX` rewritten to produce the three required sheets: **Resumen** (total de cambios, usuarios activos, cambios por tipo, filtros, firma SHA-256), **Detalles** (timestamp, usuario, acción, entidad, valores anterior/nuevo, IP — one log per row), and **Usuarios** (activity per user). Numbers are written as real numeric cells and timestamps as real Excel date cells with a consistent `yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss` format, so the file is ready for pivots and filtering. The PDF path now shows the username alongside the ID.

No frontend changes were needed — `core/Admin.js#exportAuditReport` already downloads the xlsx blob from the admin page, and the controller already enforced the `VIEW_AUDIT_LOGS` permission and correct headers.

Verification: I exercised the generator directly with sample data and re-read the resulting buffer with `XLSX.read` — three sheets with the correct names, numeric cells typed `n`, date cells typed `d` with the consistent format, and `generatePDF` still produces a valid PDF. Frontend `npm run lint` and `npm run typecheck` pass; backend lint/typecheck are the configured no-ops. Learnings appended to `.ralph-tui/progress.md`, including a new codebase pattern on server-side SheetJS exports with typed cells. No commits made, per the workflow.

One note for the future: the export uses `findMany` with no limit, so a very large audit table would load entirely into memory — pagination/streaming is noted in progress.md as a possible follow-up.

